182	/**
183	 * Resolves the implementation to use, this uses typical multiple dispatching
184	 * methods of finding the best matching implementation or resolver.
185	 * (Based on `isinstance()`, the knowledge that non-abstract DTypes cannot
186	 * be subclassed is used, however.)
187	 *
188	 * NOTE: This currently does not take into account output dtypes which do not
189	 *       have to match.  The possible extension here is that if an output
190	 *       is given (and thus an output dtype), but not part of the signature
191	 *       we could ignore it for matching, but *prefer* a loop that matches
192	 *       better.
193	 *       Why is this not done currently?  First, it seems a niche feature that
194	 *       loops can only be distinguished based on the output dtype.  Second,
195	 *       there are some nasty theoretical things because:
196	 *
197	 *            np.add(f4, f4, out=f8)
198	 *            np.add(f4, f4, out=f8, dtype=f8)
199	 *
200	 *       are different, the first uses the f4 loop, the second the f8 loop.
201	 *       The problem is, that the current cache only uses the op_dtypes and
202	 *       both are `(f4, f4, f8)`.  The cache would need to store also which
203	 *       output was provided by `dtype=`/`signature=`.
204	 *
205	 * @param ufunc
206	 * @param op_dtypes The DTypes that are either passed in (defined by an
207	 *        operand) or defined by the `signature` as also passed in as
